```cpp
template <typename hb_serialize_context_t>
hb_array_t copy (hb_serialize_context_t *c) const
{
    TRACE_SERIALIZE (this);
    auto* out = c->start_embed (arrayZ);
    if (unlikely (!c->extend_size (out, get_size ()))) return_trace (hb_array_t ());
    for (unsigned i = 0; i < length; i++)
      out[i] = arrayZ[i];
    return_trace (hb_array_t (out, length));
}
```

`start_embed(arrayZ)` uses the source pointer `arrayZ` as a template parameter to determine the type, but it actually just returns `this->head` (the current write position). So that's fine.

`extend_size(out, get_size())` where `get_size()` returns `length * sizeof(Type)`. For `HBUINT8`, `sizeof` is 1, so `get_size()` returns `length`. The `extend_size` function checks that the new size doesn't exceed the buffer.

Then the loop copies `length` elements from `arrayZ` to `out`. If `length` is very large, this would read past the end of the source buffer.

But the question is: how can `length` (which is `data_length`) be very large? It's computed as `delta - SBIXGlyph::min_size`, where `delta = imageOffsetsZ[old_gid + 1] - imageOffsetsZ[old_gid]`. And `imageOffsetsZ[old_gid + 1]` is checked to be within `available_len`.

